%% Lifecycle of the two account keys, the SAS tokens signed from them and the AD object kerb keys - and the one ordering that causes an outage
%% Source: codex/runbook-storage-key-rotation-and-shared-key-incidents.md classes 1, 2, 5 and 6 and codex/playbook-harden-existing-storage-account.md step 4; Microsoft Learn - Manage account access keys (rotation order, keyCreationTime, key expiration policy, SAS revocation, listkeys roles); Enable AD DS authentication for Azure Files
stateDiagram-v2
  direction LR

  state "The two 512-bit account keys - key1 primary and key2 secondary" as PAIR {
    direction TB
    state "Never rotated - keyCreationTime is null on both keys. A key expiration policy CANNOT be created in this state" as VIRGIN
    state "key1 carries every key-based client, key2 is the idle standby. Microsoft rule - use ONE key everywhere, never key1 here and key2 there" as STEADY1
    state "Clients repointed to key2. key1 is now unused but still valid" as CUTOVER
    state "key1 regenerated. Every account SAS and service SAS signed with the old key1 is dead at that instant" as ROT1
    state "Clients repointed to the new key1. key2 idle again" as STEADY2
    state "key2 regenerated. Both keys now carry a keyCreationTime" as ROT2
    state "keyPolicy keyExpirationPeriodInDays is 180. The portal shows a rotation reminder and built-in policy 044985bb reports compliant" as POLICY
    state "OUTAGE - the key in use was regenerated first. Every key-based mount, backup job and SAS fails at once" as OUTAGE

    [*] --> VIRGIN
    VIRGIN --> STEADY1
    STEADY1 --> CUTOVER : step 1 repoint clients to key2
    CUTOVER --> ROT1 : step 2 regenerate key1
    ROT1 --> STEADY2 : step 3 repoint clients to the new key1
    STEADY2 --> ROT2 : step 4 regenerate key2
    ROT2 --> POLICY : both keys rotated once, so the policy is settable at last
    POLICY --> CUTOVER : the next 180-day cycle restarts at step 1
    STEADY1 --> OUTAGE : regenerating the key clients hold, with no repoint first
    OUTAGE --> CUTOVER : repoint clients to the untouched key, then resume the order
  }

  state "Credentials derived from an account key" as DERIVED {
    direction TB
    state "Account SAS - signed with key1 or key2" as ACCSAS
    state "Service SAS - signed with key1 or key2" as SVCSAS
    state "Revoked the instant its signing key is regenerated. It must be reissued from the new key" as DEAD
    state "User delegation SAS - signed with Entra credentials and immune to key rotation, but Blob Storage ONLY, so a file share cannot use one" as UDSAS

    [*] --> ACCSAS
    [*] --> SVCSAS
    ACCSAS --> DEAD : signing key regenerated
    SVCSAS --> DEAD : signing key regenerated
    DEAD --> ACCSAS : reissue from the new key
    UDSAS --> UDSAS : never an option for Azure Files
  }

  state "kerb1 and kerb2 - the Kerberos keys of the AD DS join, rotated on their own clock" as KERB {
    direction TB
    state "The contoso.local computer object in OU=AzureFiles holds the password derived from kerb1" as KERB1
    state "Rotated to kerb2 with Update-AzStorageAccountADObjectPassword" as KERB2
    state "Password expired under OU policy, or RC4 disabled in the domain before the object moved to AES-256. Mounts fail with error 1396 or KRB_AP_ERR_MODIFIED" as KFAIL

    [*] --> KERB1
    KERB1 --> KERB2 : rotate, alternating each cycle
    KERB2 --> KERB1 : rotate back on the next cycle
    KERB1 --> KFAIL : OU password expiry was left enabled
    KFAIL --> KERB2 : rotate the object password, Set-ADComputer AES256, klist purge on the clients
  }

  note right of KERB
    Three rotations, three blast radii, no overlap.
    Regenerating key1 or key2 does not touch kerb1 or kerb2,
    and rotating the AD object password does not touch the
    account keys. Only an account key rotation revokes SAS.
    Listing or regenerating either kind of key needs
    Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/listkeys/action or
    regeneratekey/action, carried by Owner, Contributor and
    Storage Account Key Operator Service Role - three roles
    Contoso should count and name.
  end note
